    "title": "Elite linebacker recruit decommits from Georgia football - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Owen Hillman ,\n18 hours ago Georgia linebacker Chris Cole (9) reacts following a tackle during the third quarter against Auburn By Dale Zanine, IMAGN IMAGES via Reuters Connect The Georgia Bulldogs have lost a linebacker commit from the 2027 recruiting class. Linebacker Temorris Campbell Jr. decommitted from the Georgia Bulldogs, according to Zach Bolstein of 247Sports.\nCampbell is a three-star in the class of 2027. The 6-foot-1, 210-pound LB plays for Miami Carol City High School in Miami, Florida. He is the No. 60 linebacker prospect in the nation and the No. 69 prospect from the state of Florida.\nCampbell committed to the Georgia Bulldogs on May 12, and he shut down his recruitment on Oct. 2, professing Georgia was his dream school.\nAfter Campbell's decommitment, Georgia has 23 commits, ranking them as the No. 9 class in the nation. The Bulldogs still have linebacker commit Joakim Gouda, but no other linebackers. The Bulldogs have added to their commitment pool recently. They flipped Christian Mays from Tennessee and Braylon Miller from Liberty , so the recruiting momentum is still strong. UGA and coach Kirby Smart also added a commitment from four-star offensive lineman Koehn Dial .\nFollow UGA Wire on Instagram , Facebook , X (formerly Twitter) or Threads for more Georgia football coverage!\nThis article originally appeared on UGA Wire: Elite linebacker recruit decommits from Georgia football\n1",
    "summary": "18 hours ago Georgia linebacker Chris Cole (9) reacts following a tackle during the third quarter against Auburn By Dale Zanine, IMAGN IMAGES via Reuters Connect The Georgia Bulldogs have lost a linebacker commit from the 2027 recruiting class. They flipped Christian Mays from Tennessee and Braylon Miller from Liberty , so the recruiting momentum is still strong. UGA and coach Kirby Smart also added a commitment from four-star offensive lineman Koehn Dial . Follow UGA Wire on Instagram , Facebook , X (formerly Twitter) or Threads for more Georgia football coverage!",
